'''Māngere''' (spelled '''Mangere''' before 1997) is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]], returning one member of parliament to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives|Representatives of New Zealand]]. The current MP for Māngere is [[Lemauga Lydia Sosene]] of the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]]. She has held this electorate since [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Lemauga Lydia Sosene |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/members-of-parliament/sosene-lemauga-lydia/ |website=www.parliament.nz |publisher=New Zealand Parliament |access-date=25 November 2023 |date=20 October 2023}}</ref>

==Population centres==
Through an amendment in the Electoral Act in 1965, the number of electorates in the [[South Island]] was fixed at 25, an increase of one since the 1962 electoral redistribution.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=108, 111, 112}} It was accepted that through the more rapid population growth in the [[North Island]], the number of its electorates would continue to increase, and to keep proportionality, three new electorates were allowed for in the 1967 electoral redistribution for the next election.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=111}} In the North Island, five electorates were newly created (including Mangere) and one electorate was reconstituted while three electorates were abolished.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=107, 111}} In the South Island, three electorates were newly created and one electorate was reconstituted while three electorates were abolished.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=108, 112}} The overall effect of the required changes was highly disruptive to existing electorates, with all but three electorates having their boundaries altered.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=111f}} These changes came into effect with the {{NZ election link|1969}}.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=111}}

Māngere is based around the [[South Auckland]] suburbs of [[Māngere]], [[Māngere Bridge (suburb)|Māngere Bridge]], [[Favona]] and [[Māngere East]]. It has existed as an electorate since 1969; its boundaries were extended ahead of the introduction of [[Mixed-member proportional representation|mixed-member proportional]] (MMP) voting in [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996]], swallowing up a section of the former [[Papatoetoe (New Zealand electorate)|Papatoetoe]] electorate.

{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Māngere.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
In the 2007 boundary redistribution, [[Papatoetoe]] and [[Middlemore]] were transferred to the {{NZ electorate link|Manukau East}} electorate.<ref name="2012 electorate profile">{{cite web |title=Mana: Electoral Profile |url= http://www.parliament.nz/resource/en-nz/DBHOH_Lib_EP_Mangere_Data_3/aa8b539a560212a3605d1d89054f83d4ff4f3447 |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |access-date=3 October 2014 |page=3 |date=September 2012}}</ref> The 2013/14 and 2020 redistributions did not change the boundaries further.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=2 October 2014 |page=9 |date=4 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006075102/http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |archive-date=6 October 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The 2025 boundary review moved an area in [[Papatoetoe]] between [[State Highway 1 (New Zealand)|State Highway 1]] and the [[North Island Main Trunk]] from {{NZ electorate link|Manurewa}} while transferring back an area in [[Wiri]].<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=22 September 2025 |page=19 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

==History==
Māngere, and all of South Auckland, forms the safest part of the Labour Party's core vote. Even during landslide elections in the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]]'s favour, such as in [[1975 New Zealand general election|1975]] and [[1990 New Zealand general election|1990]], no Labour candidate for Māngere was seriously troubled.

Māngere was first represented by [[Colin Moyle]] of the Labour Party in [[1969 New Zealand general election|1969]]. Moyle represented the electorate until his resignation in 1977 over what became known as the 'Moyle Affair', and a [[1977 Mangere by-election|subsequent by-election]] was won by a young barrister named [[David Lange]], who would become Prime Minister after Labour's [[1984 New Zealand general election|1984 election]] victory. Lange retired in 1996 and the nomination was handed to Taito Phillip Field, at the time the MP for [[Otara (New Zealand electorate)|Otara]]. Field was returned with a high share of the vote in subsequent elections, but following his expulsion from the Labour caucus in 2007, his former party nominated former [[Manukau City]] deputy mayor [[William Sio]] in his place, who won the seat with a majority of over 7,000 votes in the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 election]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Official Count Results — Māngere |url=http://2008.electionresults.govt.nz/electorate-22.html |publisher=New Zealand Electoral Commission |access-date=25 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081211164819/http://2008.electionresults.govt.nz/electorate-22.html |archive-date=11 December 2008 |url-status=dead}}</ref> In the {{NZ election link year|2011}} and {{NZ election link|2014}}s, Sio's majority was circa 15,000 votes.<ref name="Māngere 2011 election results" /><ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2014/electorate-23.html
| title=Official Count Results – Māngere
| publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]]
| access-date=4 October 2014 |date=4 October 2014}}</ref>

